Newsletter Search Search News Reviews Best New Music Features Lists Columns Video Open Navigation Menu Menu Search Search News The Killers’ Dave Keuning Survived a “Major Stroke” This Month The group’s co-founding guitarist says doctors gave him a “40% chance of survival” By Jazz Monroe November 27, 2026 Dave Keuning, 2018 (Getty Images) Comment Loader Save Story Save this story Comment Loader Save Story Save this story Dave Keuning, the Killers ’ co-founding lead guitarist, has revealed that he survived a major stroke two weeks ago. The musician wrote on Instagram that he was rushed to the hospital after a blood clot cut off circulation to the right side of his brain, leaving him blind in one eye and partially paralyzed. Doctors gave him a 40 percent chance to survive and a high likelihood of disability if he did, he wrote. “But by some miracle I am fine. And yes I can play guitar just as good as before. It was the first thing I checked when I woke up from surgery.” See his Thanksgiving post below.

Keuning has been a central figure in the Killers since the band’s formation, co-writing all their biggest hits and playing every show before taking a pause to rest in 2017. He returned to the band in 2021, having released a pair of solo albums in the interim.

